What was changed, exactly
-------------------------
Only the asset paths. In each page, every `/test-assets/…` reference — in `src`
and in `srcset` alike, directory component included — is replaced by a name from
a single pinned scheme:

    /img/a.png   the W3C logo        (2 cases)
    /img/b.png   the Nyhavn photo    (4 cases)
    /img/c.png   the bread photo     (1 case)

One name per DISTINCT IMAGE, never one per case. A per-case index would
manufacture a distinguishing token the originals never had, and would defeat the
prompt-level twin rule: two prompts that are informationally identical would
differ by one digit and a byte comparison would call them distinct.

Nothing else is touched — `alt`, `srcset` descriptors, `lang`, whitespace and
the doctype are byte-exact, and the pixels each page renders are the same bytes
under a new name.

Why the whole path goes, not just the filename
-----------------------------------------------
Two cues survive a filename-only rewrite, and both decide cases:

  * `1ff696703e…`'s `srcset` retains the literal tokens `nyhavn` and `paris`;
  * the directory `/test-assets/image-filename-as-accessible-name-9eb3f6/`
    spells out the deprecated rule's own deciding criterion on five of the seven
    cases, and partitions the pool by rule while it is at it.

The `.png` extension is decorative and is wrong on purpose
-----------------------------------------------------------
Two of the three images are JPEG. A uniform name that carries no information —
not even the format — is the point, and it is safe only because nothing reads
it: Chromium sniffs an `<img>` from its bytes, and `scanner.served_content_type`
sniffs them too. **Never derive a media type from these names.** An image sent
to a multimodal model travels as `data:<media-type>;base64,…` and is decoded by
that declared type, so a `.png` label on JPEG bytes would be a lie told to the
model.

What the gold means here
------------------------
The judgment scored over this set is the WCAG 1.1.1 call — does this accessible
name describe this image? The `alt` text is unchanged and the rendered pixels are
unchanged, so neither side of that question moved and the labels carry over from
`../act-image/expected_image.json` unaltered.

What does NOT carry over is the deprecated rule's own applicability: `9eb3f6` is
about an accessible name that *is* the filename, and after ablation no name is a
filename. That rule outcome is a property of the page ACT published; it is not
what this set scores, and no report over this set may claim it is.

How this copy is pinned
-----------------------
By content hash, one line per DERIVED byte in `checksums.sha256` — the seven
pages and the three assets (verify with `shasum -a 256 -c checksums.sha256` from
this directory). `expected_image_opaque.json` and `permutation.json` are
regenerated from those bytes and are excluded, as the other derived manifests in
this repo are.

`permutation.json` is a PRE-REGISTRATION: the wrong image each case is shown in
the mismatched condition, frozen before any verdict over this set existed. It is
authored over ACT testcase ids, every case receives an image that is not its own
(asserted at build time), and it is not to be edited after a run.

